#!/bin/sh
# pg-restore — Restores the data directory from the backup repository, either to the
# end of the archived WAL or to a point in time, into an empty data volume. Run it in a
# one-off container of the database service after removing the old volume:
#
#   docker compose down
#   docker volume rm <data volume>
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps --entrypoint pg-restore <db> latest
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps --entrypoint pg-restore <db> "2026-10-18 12:00:00+00"
#   docker compose up -d
#
# On start the server replays WAL up to the target and then accepts writes.

set -e

target=${1:?usage: pg-restore latest | "<timestamp with time zone>"}
if [ "$target" = latest ]; then
    set --
else
    set -- --type=time "--target=$target" --target-action=promote
fi

if [ -n "$(ls -A "$PGDATA" 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
    echo "pg-restore: $PGDATA is not empty; remove the data volume first" >&2
    exit 1
fi
# A new volume is created root-owned; the server and pgBackRest run as postgres.
mkdir -p "$PGDATA"
chown postgres:postgres "$PGDATA"
chmod 700 "$PGDATA"
exec gosu postgres pgbackrest --stanza=main "$@" restore
